#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105612 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105760 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ba3182f3-3b70-5d65-bbb3-6a5e163b3a1a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Points of Intersection of Two Circles Where One Is Centered at the Origin"}] :question/id #uuid "e17be858-3226-5845-b0c9-f3cf86987ee6" :question/math-academy-id "e-5395" :question/problem "The circles circles $r=-4\\cos \\theta$ and $r=2$ intersect at points $A$ and $B$, as shown below. What are the polar coordinates of the point $B$?\n\n![](images/5d/5d7d5084d0b13c7484acdc8796bcd8584d77351befacacf84a153430b34f6fd4.png)" :question/worked-solution "To determine the polar coordinates of the intersection points, we will solve the following system of equations for $r$ and $\\theta$:\n\n$$\n{r =-4\\cos θ; r = 2\n$$\n\nWe begin by setting the equations equal to each other and solving for $\\theta$. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-4\\cos θ &= 2 \\\\\n\\cos θ &= -\\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation in the interval $[0,360^\\circ)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\theta =120^\\circ\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\theta = 240^\\circ\n$$\n\nAlso, notice that $r=2$ at both intersection points.\n\nTherefore, the polar coordinates of the point $B$ are\n\n$$\n(r,\\theta)=\\big(2, \\, 240^\\circ\\big)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105614 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105762 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "64910a32-96b4-59c4-8f61-84e7c532e59d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Points of Intersection of a Limaçon and Another Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "d58288cb-2983-5927-8436-8dbd39d6f67d" :question/math-academy-id "e-5397" :question/problem "Find the polar coordinates of the intersection points of the polar curves $r =3+3\\cos\\theta$ and $r = 4$, shown below. Round your answers to $1$ decimal place where appropriate.\n\n![](images/cb/cbdc7d93d48f21d3d4ae67a5cb7692801d240e896263a137557e67f8a5bfbcfc.png)" :question/worked-solution "To determine the polar coordinates of the intersection points, we will solve the following system of equations for $r$ and $\\theta$:\n\n$$\n{r = 3 + 3\\cos θ; r = 4\n$$\n\nWe begin by setting the equations equal to each other and solving for $\\theta$. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 3+3\\cos \\theta & = 4 \\\\[5pt] 3\\cos \\theta & = 1 \\\\[3pt] \\cos \\theta & = \\dfrac 1 3. \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation in the interval $[0^\\circ,360^\\circ)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\theta_1 \\approx 70.5^{\\circ}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\theta_2 \\approx 289.5\n$$\n\nrounded to $1$ decimal place.\n\nNotice that we must have\n$r=4$\nat any of the points of intersection.\n\nTherefore, the polar coordinates of the points of intersection are\n\n$$\n(r,\\theta)=\\left(4, 70.5 \\, ^\\circ \\right)\\qquad \\textrm{and} \\qquad (r,\\theta)=\\left(4, 289.5 \\, ^\\circ\\right)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105615 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105763 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "179ed9f0-7568-5cb0-8f06-626e1e494b62" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Points of Intersection Between a Circle and Another Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "716ee2b8-17d2-5754-a09d-cec35808df9d" :question/math-academy-id "e-5399" :question/problem "The circles $r=4\\cos \\theta$ and $r=4\\sqrt{3}\\sin \\theta$ intersect at points $O$ and $A$, as shown in the diagram. What are the polar coordinates of the point $A$?\n\n![](images/27/2766090535f1931a0cb15a248eb62c9b1dc5b5f9bc15b6dff5d544876527d77c.png)" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we notice from the graph that\n\n$$\n(r,\\theta)=\\left(0, 0\\right)\n$$\n\nis an obvious first point of intersection.\n\nTo find the second point of intersection, we set the equations of the curves equal to each other and solve for $\\theta$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4\\sqrt{3}\\sin θ &= 4\\cos θ \\\\\n\\frac{\\sin θ}{\\cos θ} &= \\frac{4}{4\\sqrt{3}} \\\\\n\\tan θ &= \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the solution\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\n$$\n\nin the $1$st quadrant.\n\nNow, we use the equation\n\n$$\nr=4\\cos \\theta\n$$\n\nto find the $r$-coordinate of the point $A$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr &= 4\\cos (\\frac{π}{6}) \\\\\n&= 4 \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\\n&= 2\\sqrt{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, polar coordinates of the point $A$ are\n\n$$\n(r,\\theta)=\\left(2\\sqrt{3}, \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\right)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106318 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105760 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ba3182f3-3b70-5d65-bbb3-6a5e163b3a1a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Points of Intersection of Two Circles Where One Is Centered at the Origin"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9b0b64b3-a092-57da-98ac-b09a956d0f85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(2, \\frac{7π}{6})$ and $(2, \\frac{11π}{6})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104057} {:answer/id #uuid "e75a3fd4-bee7-577c-9306-e120eba00e80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(\\sqrt{3}, \\frac{4π}{3})$ and $(\\sqrt{3}, \\frac{5π}{3})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104058} {:answer/id #uuid "f109fb00-91fe-545a-8633-83a8c706e6f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(\\sqrt{3}, \\frac{5π}{4})$ and $(\\sqrt{3}, \\frac{7π}{4})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104059} {:answer/id #uuid "2412a9c4-8e9e-5a7f-8443-e84480e4b151" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(2, \\frac{6π}{5})$ and $(2, \\frac{9π}{5})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104060} {:answer/id #uuid "2661cdd2-9daa-5344-8ce3-2210aac66fe7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(\\sqrt{3}, \\frac{7π}{6})$ and $(\\sqrt{3}, \\frac{11π}{6})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104061}] :answer-field/id #uuid "aaacbcc3-32aa-5508-97fd-b5877b56f2cd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104056}] :question/id #uuid "4d89c533-9b96-541f-8ca2-288a33c48339" :question/math-academy-id "q-42494" :question/problem "![](images/2e/2ee08e17ee0f474a675e83275c290a29a0f2814e5277694a5629282cfd861d56.png)\n\nFind the polar coordinates for the intersection points of the circles $r =-2\\sin θ$ and $r = \\sqrt{3}$, shown above."}]}
